From 77906e4a0427bc885f8dff5690f413ca808eb202 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 07:37:01 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 14/24] Watch the guest's wire count on type-11 surfaces, and
 say when it drops

Two macOS 27.2 panics read back out of guest RAM had a mapped display swap
buffer's surface descriptor at a wire count of zero while it was still in
the mapper's wired set; its pages were compressed and reused, and the next
full-frame write into the mapping landed on kernel memory. wire_mapping
prepares both descriptors before MAP and unwire_mapping completes them only
after UNMAP succeeds, so something else completed it.

With REIMS_VGPU_WIRE_WATCH on, the device reads _wireCount of the surface
and page-table descriptors of every type-11 mapping: on MAP, before UNMAP,
before every host write into the mapping, around every display present
packet and after the stamp that completes it, and from the worker every
5 ms for swap buffers and every 100 ms for all. Each change is a
`wire_watch transition` line naming the last swap, stamp and write. The
first zero, negative or below-MAP value is `wire_watch first_anomaly`, with
whether anything else in the descriptor moved, its mixed-data length,
whether this device ever wrote the descriptor's frame, and the sixteen
swap, present, stamp, hold and map events before it. Display presents with
their payload words, translation holds, swaps seen waiting at a held FIFO's
head, and the stamps that complete swaps are `wire_watch_ev` lines.

Nothing is read on trust. The kernel's Mach-O header is found below
VBAR_EL1 and its version string must be 27.2's exactly, or the watch is off
for the boot. A MappingInternal must name its id, size and the page count
this device resolved, its page-table descriptor's buffer must be the table
this device reads, the surface descriptor's length must be the mapping's
size, and both descriptors must be of one kernel class; otherwise
`wire_watch_layout_unverified` and the mapping is not watched. Off a vCPU it
reads only through a validated kernel walk.

REIMS_VGPU_WIRE_GUARD, with the watch, refuses a host CPU write into a
mapping whose checked surface count reads zero or less, and keeps an owed
frame owed. A positive count is not a permission: the guest can complete
the descriptor between the read and the write.

Not run on a guest. The tests put a fake 27.2 kernel image and mapping in
FakeHost memory; one drives a DisplaySwap through drain_pending.

